<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n7217" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">cautēla</span></b> , ae, f.
		cautus, caveo (mostly post - class.), <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>caution</i>, <i>precaution.</i>
		</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> In gen.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">si minus cum curā aut
					cautelā locus loquendi lectus est,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plaut.
					Mil. 3, 1, 6</span></i>
			 dub. (bracketed by Brix); <i><span class="en">App. M. 2,
				p. 117, 7</span></i>; <i><span class="en">5</span></i>, p. 160,
			36.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> Esp., in
			the jurists, = cautio, <i>a surety</i>, <i>security</i>, <i><span class="en">Dig. 3, 3,
				15</span></i>; <i><span class="en">13, 7,
			6</span></i>; <i><span class="en">16, 3,
			14</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
